my name is Cedric Necaise I'm the wine  director at Eleven Madison Park and we  are here today to talk about wine labels  why they're important what's written on  them and how it relates to what's in the  bottle so we're gonna start with  something that everyone is familiar with  which is California Cabernet starting at  the very top of the label and then  working down so vintage 2010 important  note about vintage is that it is when  the grapes were picked or harvested not  when the wine was released or actually  bottled it's really important to know  your own taste some people really like  young very full very kind of up front  wines and some people like older wines  are a little more subtle a little bit  earthy err and less tannic the actual  age of the wine matters less than  actually knowing what happened during  that vintage so if it was warm vintage  wines a riper it's cool vintage wines  are less ripe and lower in alcohol there  are some times where there's fires in a  wine region which will affect the grapes  if it rained a lot all those things are  much more important than your personal  preference of liking older wine or  younger wine then moving down to the  producer in this case the Knightsbridge  producer in of Cabernet Sauvignon the  varietal here on the label and then  below that the winery itself in Knights  Valley and Knights Valley an area in  California those two or those three  things put together is a terroir terroir  is the summation of everything that goes  into making a bottle of wine the type of  soil that is grown on the amount of rain  that falls the amount of sunshine to the  heat during the day and heat at night  the type of fertilizer anything that can  go into producing a bottle line is the  terroir of the wine lastly on this label  is the alcohol percentage generally the  higher the alcohol the riper the grapes  the sweeter the fruit the richer the  wine will be so in this case 14.9%  alcohol you know that's a pretty full  body pretty rich velvety flavored wine  with lots of fruit alcohol is certainly  the most important indicator for how  full-bodied a wine will be so you see  with 16% alcohol and label you shouldn't  automatically know that it's very  full-bodied very rich not just high in  alcohol
 

